WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
Updating application images does not patch the platform beneath them.

00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:15.802
Kubernetes nodes have operating systems, kernels, container runtimes, agents, drivers, and management components with separate vulnerabilities and release cycles.

00:00:15.802 --> 00:00:21.480
Control-plane components and cluster add-ons also require inventory and maintenance.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.665
Node updates often require cordoning, draining, replacement, or restart.

00:00:26.665 --> 00:00:35.307
Workloads must tolerate rescheduling through replicas, readiness checks, graceful shutdown, storage design, and realistic resource capacity.

00:00:35.307 --> 00:00:42.467
A patched node is not useful if maintenance causes an avoidable service outage.

00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:46.170
Rolling updates replace workload instances gradually.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:51.355
Deployment settings control surge, unavailable replicas, and health evaluation.

00:00:51.355 --> 00:01:01.478
Pod disruption budgets can protect minimum availability during voluntary disruption, but an unrealistic budget can also block node maintenance.

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:03.700
Cluster compatibility matters.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:12.836
Kubernetes versions, runtimes, network and storage plugins, admission systems, and application APIs must remain within supported combinations.

00:01:12.836 --> 00:01:19.996
Review version-skew policy, deprecations, vendor guidance, and rollback constraints before updating platform components.

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:23.205
Coordinate application and platform teams.

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:32.341
Application owners understand workload health and data behavior; platform teams manage nodes, scheduling, runtimes, and control-plane dependencies.

00:01:32.341 --> 00:01:38.513
Security provides prioritization and verifies that exposed versions were actually removed.

00:01:38.513 --> 00:01:46.661
For managed services, clarify which layers the provider patches and which remain the customer's responsibility.

00:01:46.661 --> 00:01:57.771
Test maintenance in representative environments, monitor workloads and cluster health, retain recovery procedures, and verify node and component inventory after rollout.
